---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="About Omoshola Owolabi. Enterprise supply-chain systems engineering, independent software, AI research and the connections between them."
  profilePage={true}
>
  <main id="main-content" class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-4 pb-12">
    <section
      id="about"
      class="prose mb-28 mt-8 max-w-3xl prose-img:border-0"
    >
      <!-- Hero -->
      <div class="not-prose mb-10">
        <img
          src="/omoshola-working.jpg"
          alt="Omoshola Owolabi working at his desk"
          class="float-right ml-8 mb-6 w-80 rounded-xl object-cover shadow-lg"
          width="320"
        />
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl leading-tight mb-4">
          Omoshola Owolabi
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed opacity-80 max-w-xl">
          My work is enterprise supply-chain systems engineering: designing the planning
          models, data logic and analytical tools that connect demand, supply and operational
          decisions across hardware and semiconductor environments. I also build independent
          software, research AI and investigate the systems behind industrial technology.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="clear: both;"></div>

      <!-- Intro statement -->
      <div class="not-prose my-10 border-l-4 pl-6" style="border-color: var(--accent);">
        <p class="text-xl font-medium leading-relaxed">
          I turn complex supply-and-demand relationships into planning systems
          people can understand and use.
        </p>
      </div>

      <hr />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-10 mb-4">Enterprise planning, from the inside</h2>

      <p>
        A finished product hides a network of dependencies: materials, chips, suppliers,
        factories and delivery commitments. A small change in one place can reshape the
        options somewhere else. That connection between physical constraints and
        information is what makes supply-chain planning such an interesting engineering problem.
      </p>

      <p>
        At IBM, my role is Kinaxis Subject Matter Expert — Supply Chain. My work spans
        S&amp;OP and MRP, demand reconciliation, multi-level bills of materials, supply
        coverage and scenario modeling. I translate operational requirements into planning
        structures, calculation logic and analytical views that help people understand
        shortages, compare options and decide what needs attention.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-10 mb-4">Engineering the planning system</h2>

      <p>
        The model has to reflect the operation. A demand signal, an inventory balance and a
        supplier commitment can arrive from different systems, at different times, with
        different assumptions. I work on the data structures and integration logic that
        bring those inputs into a consistent planning model.
      </p>

      <p>
        A multi-level bill of materials connects a finished product to the components and
        supply commitments it depends on. My work includes calculated expressions, multi-tier
        rollups and coverage logic that make those relationships usable: where supply is
        sufficient, where a shortage emerges, and how a change travels through the plan.
      </p>

      <p>
        Scenario analysis and decision-support views bring that logic to the people using
        it. A planner needs to understand the effect of changing demand or a supply date;
        a leader needs a clear view of the trade-offs. I connect the underlying calculations
        to those questions, with attention to data integrity, traceability and assumptions.
      </p>

      <p>
        My research extends into AI decision systems, financial risk and supply-chain
        resilience. Co-authored publications explore ethical AI in financial decisions
        and machine learning for customer churn prediction. Research, implementation
        and technical review give me different ways to examine the same question:
        what would make this result worth trusting?
      </p>

      <hr />

      <figure class="not-prose my-12">
        <img
          src="/omoshola-thinking.jpg"
          alt="Omoshola Owolabi at work"
          class="mx-auto w-full max-w-xs rounded-xl object-cover shadow-lg"
          width="800"
          height="1066"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="mt-3 text-center text-sm italic opacity-70">
          Most of the interesting problems live where the technical and human sides meet.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-10 mb-4">The experience behind the work</h2>

      <p>
        I grew up in Nigeria, and my route into engineering came through trade finance,
        supply-chain operations and data science. Working inside an operation before
        modeling it taught me to ask what the data leaves out and who has to act on
        the answer.
      </p>

      <p>
        That background keeps the human side close. A planning decision changes someone's
        workload, a supplier's priorities or a customer's options. A useful explanation
        has to connect the technical result to those consequences.
      </p>

      <p>
        I also review research, judge technical competitions and mentor practitioners.
        Explaining an idea to someone approaching it from a different direction is a
        good way to discover which parts I understand and which still need work.
      </p>

      <p>
        My curiosity reaches beyond any one field: African knowledge systems, economics,
        infrastructure, manufacturing and the history of computation. I write about
        these connections when there is a mechanism to investigate, rather than a
        resemblance to take on faith.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-10 mb-4">Independent engineering and research</h2>

      <p>
        My independent engineering is separate from my employer work. It explores how
        software can retain context, explain a decision and leave a record that can
        be inspected later. AI is one part of that question; the surrounding system
        matters just as much.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Nexus</strong> explores supply-chain intelligence through probabilistic
        planning, simulation and supplier dependencies. The underlying question is
        familiar from my professional work: how can a model help someone reason about
        uncertainty without hiding it behind a single number?
      </p>

      <p>
        I also investigate the infrastructure around agents. What should an agent remember?
        How should it identify itself? Which actions should a policy permit, and what
        evidence should remain after an action? Those are software design questions
        before they become claims about intelligence.
      </p>

      <p>
        My specialist-model work includes <strong>Solen</strong> for supply chains,
        <strong>Verac</strong> for finance and settlement, and <strong>Axiom</strong>
        for financial markets. The research question is how domain knowledge and
        reasoning can improve the usefulness of a model in a specific setting.
      </p>

      <p>
        At <a href="https://github.com/agentralabs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agentra Labs</a>,
        the infrastructure projects explore memory, identity, time, policy and planning.
        <strong>AgenticMemory</strong> retains facts, decisions and reasoning across sessions.
        Related projects include <strong>AgenticIdentity</strong>, <strong>AgenticContract</strong>,
        <strong>AgenticTime</strong>, <strong>AgenticPlanning</strong>,
        <strong>AgenticVeritas</strong> and <strong>AgenticCognition</strong>.
        The repositories hold the implementation detail.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>XAP</strong> and the <a href="https://github.com/agentra-commerce/verity-engine" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>Verity Truth Engine</strong></a> explore the records
        around agent transactions: what was agreed, what happened, and what another
        party can independently check. <strong>ZexRail</strong> is part of that independent
        settlement work. Technical notes and code explain the design in more detail.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Hydra</strong> brings several of these ideas together in an agent system.
        It is a way to investigate how memory, policy and reasoning interact, rather
        than treating each component as an isolated capability.
      </p>

      <p>
        The articles on this site preserve the development of these ideas, including earlier
        approaches and experiments. Some are architecture notes, some are research,
        and some are accounts of building. Together they show the questions behind
        the code, not just a list of tools.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-10 mb-4">How I approach a problem</h2>

      <p>I think in systems.</p>
      <p>I test ideas against the domain they live in.</p>
      <p>I design for what actually breaks.</p>

      <figure class="not-prose my-12">
        <img
          src="/omoshola-focus.jpg"
          alt="Omoshola Owolabi at his desk"
          class="mx-auto w-full max-w-xs rounded-xl object-cover shadow-lg"
          width="800"
          height="1066"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="mt-3 text-center text-sm italic opacity-70">
          Most days look like this.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <hr />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-10 mb-4">Get in touch</h2>

      <p>
        I welcome conversations about enterprise planning, supply-chain systems engineering,
        AI research and independent software. I am particularly interested in problems
        where understanding the connection between fields changes the way we approach them.
      </p>

      <p>
        Reach me by <a href="mailto:owolabi.omoshola@outlook.com">email</a> or on
        <a
          href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/omosholaowolabi/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">LinkedIn</a
        >. Research output is indexed on <a href="https://orcid.org/0009-0006-4089-0732" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ORCID</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
